data.content:
The lightest approach for modern .NET (8+) is the **built-in `TimeProvider`** — no new interfaces or wrappers needed.

**How it works:**

1. Add a `TimeProvider` parameter (defaulting to `TimeProvider.System`):

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

2. In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (NuGet) for a fake:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.IsTrue(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
Assert.IsFalse(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(30)); // move time forward
Assert.IsTrue(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**
- `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL (.NET 8+) — zero production dependencies
- The optional parameter means existing callers don't break (no DI container needed)
- `FakeTimeProvider` lets you set and advance time deterministically

